Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-27
Subject: Drop the charset/codepage path from the bootstrap compiler
License: expat

The 2.2.4 compiler reaches `rtl/inc/charset.pp` and the per-codepage
unit tables (cp437.pas, cp8859_1.pas, ...) for one purpose: convert
non-UTF-8 source bytes into wide chars during lexing of widestring
literals. The bootstrap target (compiling fpc itself) has zero
widestring literals in its source tree, so the path is never
exercised at runtime -- it is link-only baggage that drags `charset`
and the cp* tables into the unit graph.

Drop the dependency entirely:
- `widestr.pas`: remove `uses charset` plus the cp* unit imports;
  collapse `asciichar2unicode` / `ascii2unicode` / `cpavailable` to
  byte-to-widechar pass-through so the surface stays callable for
  `scandir.pas`'s `{$CodePage X}` directive handling.
- `options.pas`: remove `uses charset`; drop the `-Fm <name>`
  loadunicodemapping handler since there is no longer a charset
  registry to populate.

Source files using non-UTF-8 widestring literals will silently
widen each byte to its codepoint (identity for ASCII, lossy for
non-ASCII) -- acceptable for the bootstrap, which never produces
such input. The post-bootstrap compiler can be rebuilt against
upstream-pristine sources to recover full charset support.

